course: biology
lesson: 349
title: A year on a farm: lambing, haymaking, harvest and winter feeding
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: A farm's year turns with the seasons. In late winter and spring the farmer stays up all night in the lambing shed, because ewes often have twins and some lambs get stuck or are born weak. Within about half an hour a healthy lamb is standing and drinking.
explain: In early summer the sheep are sheared, and in June the grass is cut, dried in the sun, turned and baled as hay, or wrapped airtight in black plastic so it pickles into silage. Make hay while the sun shines, because wet hay goes mouldy.
explain: In autumn the tractor ploughs and sows winter wheat or barley, with gulls and rooks following to snatch the worms. In winter the cows live in big sheds, eating the hay and silage saved in summer, and the muck from the sheds goes back on the fields in spring.
explain: So nothing is wasted: summer grass becomes winter food, winter muck becomes spring fertiliser, and the next year starts again.
fact: Most lambs are born in late winter or spring; ewes often have twins and sometimes triplets, the farmer helps if a lamb is stuck or weak, and lambs stand and drink within about half an hour.
fact: A lamb whose mother has no milk is fed by bottle and then follows people around; a spare lamb can be given to a ewe that lost hers if it is rubbed with her lamb's smell.
fact: Sheep are sheared in early summer, a good shearer taking about two minutes per sheep, because most sheep are bred to keep growing wool and would get too hot and dirty.
fact: In 2015 a runaway sheep in Australia named Chris was found with a fleece weighing about 40 kilograms.
fact: Haymaking is cutting grass, drying it in the sun, turning it and baling it for winter; wet hay goes mouldy, and damp hay packed in a barn can get hot and even catch fire.
fact: Silage is grass wrapped up airtight so bacteria turn its sugars into acid and pickle it, keeping it fresh for months, and cows love it.
fact: Straw is the dry stalks left after grain is harvested and is used for bedding, while hay is dried grass used as food; straw has very little food value.
fact: In autumn farmers plough and sow winter wheat or barley, and gulls and rooks follow the plough to snatch worms and grubs.
fact: Changing the crop in each field from year to year is called crop rotation and keeps the soil healthy.
fact: In winter many cows live indoors eating hay and silage because fields are muddy and grass hardly grows, and a milking cow can drink about 50 to 100 litres of water a day.
fact: Farmers spread manure and slurry, a runny mix of muck and water, on fields as fertiliser, but too much washed into rivers harms fish, so there are rules about when to spread it.
fact: Farms have dangers such as tractors, bulls, cows with calves and deep slurry pits; the Countryside Code asks people to leave gates as they found them and keep dogs under control.
fact: If cows with calves chase a walker with a dog, the advice is to let go of the dog so both can get away.
exercise: When are most lambs born? | In late winter or spring | In the hottest weeks of midsummer | In late autumn, after the harvest | Any day of the year equally
exercise: Why does a sheep farmer stay up at night in the lambing season? | To help ewes whose lambs are stuck or weak | To count the stars, because they tell him what the weather will do | To keep foxes away from the hay | To shear the sheep in the cool
exercise: How soon can a healthy lamb stand and drink? | Within about half an hour | Not until it is about a week old | Not until it is a month or so old | Only after it is bottle-fed
exercise: How can a farmer get a ewe that lost her lamb to accept a spare one? | Rub the spare lamb with the smell of her own lamb | Paint the spare lamb exactly the same colour as her lost one | Leave them in the dark for a month | Feed the ewe extra hay
exercise: Why do bottle-fed lambs follow people around? | They think people are their mothers | They want to be sheared as soon as possible | They are herding the people like sheepdogs | They are scared of other sheep
exercise: About how long does a good shearer take for one sheep? | About two minutes | Around two hours or more | About two whole days | Only about two seconds
exercise: Why must most sheep be sheared? | They keep growing wool and would get too hot and dirty | The wool falls off on its own in summer and would be lost in the fields | Shearing makes them grow taller | It stops them from eating too much
exercise: What is haymaking? | Cutting grass, drying it, turning it and baling it | Pressing the wheat from the fields into loaves of bread | Growing grass in pots indoors | Burning old grass in winter
exercise: Why can damp hay in a barn be dangerous? | It can heat up inside and even catch fire | It slowly sets hard and turns into something like concrete | It attracts wolves | It makes the barn float
exercise: What is silage? | Grass wrapped airtight so it pickles | Grass laid out and dried in the hot sun | The dry stalks left lying after the harvest | Dried cow muck stacked up for burning
exercise: What is straw mainly used for on a farm? | Bedding for animals | The main food for cows | Fuel to run the tractors | Feeding the bees in winter
exercise: Why do gulls follow a ploughing tractor? | To snatch worms and grubs turned up by the blades | To ride along on the warm engine and rest their wings | They are trained by the farmer | They think the tractor is a ship
exercise: What is crop rotation? | Changing the crop in each field from year to year | Spinning the seeds round and round in a drum before planting | Turning the hay in the sun | Moving cows from shed to shed
exercise: Why do many cows stay indoors in winter? | Fields are muddy and the grass hardly grows | Cows hibernate and sleep right through the winter | Cows are afraid of snow | The milk would freeze solid inside their udders
exercise: About how much water can a milking cow drink in a day? | About 50 to 100 litres | About one cup | About 2 litres | About 1000 litres
exercise: Why are there rules about when farmers spread muck? | Too much washed into rivers can harm fish | Muck turns the grass purple if it is spread in the wrong month | Muck can only be spread at night | The smell is against the law on Sundays
exercise: What does the Countryside Code ask you to do with a gate? | Leave it as you found it | Always leave it wide open | Climb over it to save time | Tie it shut with baler string
test: In Shaun the Sheep, the flock dances about on two legs in thick fleece all summer. What would a real farmer do with a woolly sheep in early summer? | Shear it, so it does not get too hot and dirty | Leave the wool on forever | Shear it in midwinter | Wash it in the bath every week
test: Count them: 6 ewes each have twins. How many lambs, and how many lamb legs? | 12 lambs and 48 legs | 6 lambs and 24 legs | 12 lambs and 24 legs | 18 lambs and 72 legs
test: «forecast says rain tomorrow and the grass is cut» What would a hay farmer do? | Hurry to turn and bale the hay before the rain | Wait until after the rain has stopped and then cut some more | Leave it to soak, because rain is good for hay | Cover the field with straw
test: «hay, straw, same thing innit» How could you tell them apart? | Hay is dried grass, greener and sweet-smelling food; straw is golden, dry stalks for bedding | They really are exactly the same | Straw is green and hay is golden | Hay is only for horses, straw is only for cows
test: How is silage like pickled cucumbers? | Both are kept without air so bacteria make acid that stops them rotting | Both are laid out and dried in the sun until all their water is gone and they go crisp | Both are boiled in sugar | Both are frozen
test: Count them: a herd of 100 cows each drinks 80 litres in a day. How much is that? | 8000 litres | 800 litres | 180 litres | 80000 litres
test: «stinks out here, why's the farmer covering the field in poo» What is the reason? | Manure feeds the soil with the things that plants need to grow | He is trying to scare the walkers away so they stop crossing his land | It is a way of getting rid of rubbish | It keeps the snow off
test: Why would a farmer not spread muck just before heavy rain? | The rain could wash it into rivers and harm fish | Rain makes muck smell sweeter | Muck turns to ice in rain | The tractor would float away
test: «left the gate open, who cares, it's just a field» What could happen? | The animals could get out onto the road or into the wrong field | Nothing, because farm animals never wander far from where they are kept | The gate would rust shut | The field would grow faster
test: Cows with calves start running at you and your dog in a field. What is the advice? | Let go of the dog so you can both get away | Pick the dog up and stand still | Run towards the cows shouting | Hide behind the calves
test: In a picture book, the farmer hands every animal a sandwich for winter. What do real farm cows eat in a winter shed? | Hay and silage saved from the summer | Sandwiches and cake, just like in the picture book | Nothing, they hibernate | Fresh grass grown in the snow
test: Why would a seagull follow a tractor far from the sea? | Ploughing turns up worms and grubs to eat | It is lost and is following the noise of the engine home | Tractors carry fish | Seagulls nest on tractors
exam: Identify the principal reason for which sheep of most breeds must be shorn annually. | Such breeds continue to grow wool and would otherwise become overheated and soiled | Their wool would otherwise be shed and lost | Shearing promotes the growth of the animal in height | Shearing is required for the identification of the animal
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises silage? | Grass sealed from the air so that bacteria convert its sugars into acid, preserving it for months | Grass dried in the sun and compressed into bales | The dry stalks remaining after the harvest of grain | Manure stored over winter for use as fertiliser
exam: Ascertain the reason for which many dairy cattle are housed indoors during winter. | The fields are waterlogged and the growth of grass is negligible | Cattle are obliged to hibernate in darkness throughout the coldest months of the year | Milk would otherwise freeze within the udder | Cattle cannot tolerate the sight of snow
exam: Select the response consistent with the guidance of the Countryside Code. | Gates should be left as they were found and dogs kept under control | Gates should be left open in all circumstances | Dogs may chase sheep provided they do not bite | Walkers should feed farm animals whatever they carry
